# DEUS Character Map Sprite Standard (v2, armor looks; draft for Owner approval)

> **Retitled 2026-09-26 16:45 CT** (was "DEUS Class Map Sprite Standard" v1; backup `DEUS_CLASS_SPRITE_STANDARD_pre_1644_backup.md`). The file name is kept so existing references resolve. **Owner ruling 16:44 CT supersedes the per-class / 216-sprite plan:** map sprites are per **race × sex × armor look**.

Written 2026-09-26, 16:29–16:50 CT; v2 (armor looks, 8-direction animations) 16:45 CT. **0 generations spent** (balance 9,741.1; hard stop 9,740).
Companion files: `DEUS_GENERATION_RECIPES.md` §3.0 (pointer), `PIXELLAB_DOCS_REFERENCE.md`, look-test page `look/LOOK_COMPARISON.html`.

## Summary (for the Owner)
Every character map sprite is **one PixelLab v3 character per race × sex × armor look (9 × 2 × 5 = 90)**. The five looks are **unarmored, robe, light, medium, heavy**. They are drawn in the look of look-test row 6 (the plain v3 human, L09), at the heights in our repo size chart. Each one is made the same way:
1. A **base** character (8 directions) with idle and walk.
2. Five **States** made from that base: attack, cast, ranged, down, working. Each State gets one animation.
3. **Every animation is made in all 8 directions** (Owner 16:41 CT). Frame counts: idle 4, walk 4, attack/cast/ranged/down/working 6.

There are no per-class and no commoner sprite sets; classes may look alike (a fighter and a paladin in plate share the heavy look). **Unarmored** covers commoners and villagers; **robe** covers casters. The map sprite reflects the **worn armor category** only. Weapons, shields and other gear never change it, and each look carries a fixed PM-default weapon (§5). **Class shows in the portrait and the selected-unit panel**, not on the map. Moods use emote balloons, conditions use shared overlays, and people differ through their 144×144 faceset portraits. The race-theme block (colours, materials, motifs) applies to all five looks of a race.

Every prompt is filled from a fixed template. The slots take the chart heights, head/torso/leg pixel counts, anchor points, per-material hex ramps and weapon lengths. Because the look test showed PixelLab treats these numbers as hints, every output is also cropped, anchored and palette-snapped locally, then measured against the chart before it is accepted.

Estimated cost is **~158–198 gens per sprite first pass (~193–243 with retries), ~14,200–17,800 for all 90 (~17,400–21,900 with retries)**, well inside the ~40k the Owner accepted. The PixelLab balance (9,741 against a hard stop of 9,740) still has no usable budget, so production needs a top-up and Owner sign-off (§9).

---

